Over the last ten years, drones have become increasingly integrated with our
society: drones film our sports; they inspect our crops and survey our
wilderness; drones map out disaster sites.
Across these domains, drones are key because they are particularly adept at
maneuvering cameras and sensors to ideal vantage points in diverse environments.
However, these applications still often involve either manual operation or
extensive operator interaction, and the teams deploying these drones may consist
of more than one operator per robot.
Bridging this gap will require both more effective coordination between robots
and better understanding of application domains.

My work focuses on enabling aerial robots to make intelligent decisions about
how to sense, sample, and observe their environments both individually and in
groups.
I will start this talk by discussing active perception with individual
robots as in the challenging task of searching for survivors in a subterranean
environment---I will discuss how robots can quickly navigate and map such
environments with careful attention to dynamics, camera view, and interactions
between the two.
Given individual robots that we have endowed with the ability to intelligently
observe and inspect, how can we develop teams coordinate effectively and
efficiently?
Toward this end, I will turn to the problem of autonomously filming a group of
moving people such as a team sport or a dramatic performance.
By applying the rich theory of submodular and combinatorial optimziation, simple
algorithms can augment individuals that can film autonomously with the ability
to coordinate with other robots.
